% Origin: Kowalski, *Computational Logic and Human Thinking*,
%         Chapter 6 "The Meaning of Life", section 6.6, Example 17
%         ("Schedule 1 naturalisation requirements, object level").
% Last verification: no open issues (no errors/warnings; all tests pass).
%
% The book rule has TWO top-level disjunctions:
%   (residency OR crown service) AND good character AND knowledge of english
%   AND knowledge about life in the uk AND (principal home in uk OR continue
%   crown service).
% Rather than expand into 2x2 = 4 clauses, we factor each disjunction into a
% helper predicate (the residence-or-service condition; the intention
% condition), each defined by two single-disjunct clauses, then state one main
% rule. This keeps precedence unambiguous (gotcha #6).

the target language is: prolog.

the templates are:
*a person* fulfils the requirements of schedule 1 for naturalisation by 6.1.
*a person* satisfies the residence-or-service condition.
*a person* satisfies the intention condition.
*a person* fulfils the residency requirements.
*a person* fulfils the crown service requirements.
*a person* is of good character.
*a person* has sufficient knowledge of english.
*a person* has sufficient knowledge about life in the uk.
*a person* intends to make his principal home in the uk.
*a person* intends to continue in crown service.

the knowledge base naturalisation includes:

a person fulfils the requirements of schedule 1 for naturalisation by 6.1
    if the person satisfies the residence-or-service condition
    and the person is of good character
    and the person has sufficient knowledge of english
    and the person has sufficient knowledge about life in the uk
    and the person satisfies the intention condition.

a person satisfies the residence-or-service condition
    if the person fulfils the residency requirements.

a person satisfies the residence-or-service condition
    if the person fulfils the crown service requirements.

a person satisfies the intention condition
    if the person intends to make his principal home in the uk.

a person satisfies the intention condition
    if the person intends to continue in crown service.

scenario alice is:
    alice fulfils the residency requirements.
    alice is of good character.
    alice has sufficient knowledge of english.
    alice has sufficient knowledge about life in the uk.
    alice intends to make his principal home in the uk.
    fulfilment expects answers ["alice fulfils the requirements of schedule 1 for naturalisation by 6.1"].

% bob is missing good character -> does not fulfil.
scenario bob is:
    bob fulfils the residency requirements.
    bob has sufficient knowledge of english.
    bob has sufficient knowledge about life in the uk.
    bob intends to make his principal home in the uk.
    fulfilment expects answers [].

% carol qualifies via the crown-service path on both disjunctions.
scenario carol is:
    carol fulfils the crown service requirements.
    carol is of good character.
    carol has sufficient knowledge of english.
    carol has sufficient knowledge about life in the uk.
    carol intends to continue in crown service.
    fulfilment expects answers ["carol fulfils the requirements of schedule 1 for naturalisation by 6.1"].

query fulfilment is:
    which person fulfils the requirements of schedule 1 for naturalisation by 6.1.
